    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-n/q7",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-n/q7-question-1.svg\" alt=\"In a particular day, the following information is given about the 125 customers of a ladies leather goods store. • 25 customers did not buy a pair of shoes, nor a handbag, nor a belt. • No customer bought a pair of shoes and a belt. • 60 customers bought pairs of shoes. • 20 customers bought belts. • 24 customers bought a pair of shoes and a handbag. • 6 customers bought a belt and a handbag. One of these 125 customers is selected at random. a) Draw a Venn diagram to represent the above information and hence determine the probability that this customer bought ...\" style=\"--madas-width:560.0px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-n/q7-question-2.svg\" alt=\"i. ... shoes. ( ) 5 ii. ... belts. ( ) 2 iii. ... two different leather items. ( ) 2 b) If a customer bought a handbag, find the probability that she also bought shoes. ( ) 2 c) If a customer bought a handbag, find the probability that she also bought a belt. ( ) 2 d) State, with justification, two types of leather purchases which are statistically independent. ( ) 3\" style=\"--madas-width:570.7px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-n/q7-question-3.svg\" alt=\"Question diagram\" style=\"--madas-width:573.3px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-n/q7-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Fill in a Venn diagram: (a) Let S, H, B denote shoes, handbag and belt: Place 24 in S ∩H and 6 in B ∩H, with no customers in S ∩B. [B1] There are 125 −25 = 100 customers who purchased at least one item: [A1] Shoes-only and belt-only counts are 36 and 14. [A1] This leaves 100 −36 −14 −24 −6 = 20 handbag-only purchases: [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-n/q7-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(i) P(S) = 60/125 . [A1] = 0.48 (ii) P(B) = 20/125 . [M1] [A1] = 0.16 (iii) (24 + 6)/125 = 0.24 [A1] for exactly two items: [A1] (b) There are 24 + 6 + 20 = 50 [B1] handbag buyers, so P(S | H) = 24/50 . [A1] = 0.48\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-n/q7-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"(c) P(B | H) = 6/50 . [M1] [A1] = 0.12 (d) The product test gives P(S ∩H) = 24/125 = 0.192 = (60/125)(50/125) = P(S) P(H). Shoes and handbags are independent because P(S | H) = P(S) . [B1] [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 7 is 16 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "In a particular day, the following information is given about the 125 customers of a ladies leather goods store. • 25 customers did not buy a pair of shoes, nor a handbag, nor a belt. • No customer bought a pair of shoes and a belt. • 60 customers bought pairs of shoes. • 20 customers bought belts. • 24 customers bought a pair of shoes and a handbag. • 6 customers bought a belt and a handbag. One of these 125 customers is selected at random. a) Draw a Venn diagram to represent the above information and hence determine the probability that this customer bought ... i. ... shoes. ( ) 5 ii. ... belts. ( ) 2 iii. ... two different leather items. ( ) 2 b) If a customer bought a handbag, find the probability that she also bought shoes. ( ) 2 c) If a customer bought a handbag, find the probability that she also bought a belt. ( ) 2 d) State, with justification, two types of leather purchases which are statistically independent. ( ) 3 "
  },
