    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-n/q12-question-1.svg\" alt=\"rope 20° A box of mass 40 kg is held by a rope on a fixed rough inclined plane. The plane is\" style=\"--madas-width:560.0px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-n/q12-question-2.svg\" alt=\"at an angle of 20° to the horizontal. The rope is vertical and lies in a vertical plane containing a line of greatest slope of the incline plane, as shown in the figure above. The rope is modelled as a light inextensible string and the box is modelled as a particle, at the point of slipping down the plane. The coefficient of friction between the box and the plane is µ . Given that the ground friction has magnitude 70 N , determine the value of  µ . ( ) 9\" style=\"--madas-width:574.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-n/q12-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Start with a detailed diagram: Weight and tension are vertical: [B1] [A1] The horizontal component of friction, acting up the plane, balances that of the normal reaction R in the opposite direction: [M1] [A1] Hence 70 cos 20◦= R sin 20◦. [M1] [A1] The horizontal balance gives R = 70 cos 20◦/ sin 20◦\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-n/q12-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"= 70 cot 20◦ Divide the limiting friction by this reaction: As 70 = µR [A1] at limiting equilibrium, µ = tan 20◦≈0.364 . [M1] [A1] Alternative working. Resolve parallel and perpendicular to the plane, letting T denote the vertical tension: 70 + T sin 20◦= 40g sin 20◦, R + T cos 20◦= 40g cos 20◦. The first gives T = 40g −70/ sin 20◦. Substituting in the second gives R = (40g −T) cos 20◦= 70 cos 20◦/ sin 20◦, µ = 70/R = tan 20◦.\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 12 is 9 marks.</p>"
    ],
